// transform_identity — PMT Wave 5: identity transform elision
// Expected exit code: 7
//
// The `state_transform_elision` rewrite rule targets
// `StateTransform(x, L, L)` — a transform whose src_layout == dst_layout
// (an identity transform / no-op). When the optimizer is wired to feed
// state ops into the e-graph, this rule rewrites the transform to its
// input (eliminating the no-op conversion).
//
// Note: the `transform` keyword is parsed by VUMA but not yet lowered
// to a callable form. To exercise the same state-operation pipeline
// that the (future) transform elision will run on, this test uses a
// regular `fn set_x(p: State<Point>, v: u32)` that mutates p.x —
// semantically equivalent to a transform `to_set_x(p: State<Point>)
// -> State<Point>` that writes v to p.x. The state-operation lowering
// (StateInit → Alloc, StateWrite → Store, StateRead → Load) is the
// same regardless of whether the mutation is in a `fn` or a
// `transform` body.
//
// Under the *current* optimizer (which feeds only BinOps to the
// e-graph), the call is emitted as-is — but the program's observable
// behaviour is unchanged (exit 7), so this test passes either way.
// The file serves as a forward-looking test case for the wave that
// wires state ops into `equality_saturation_with_cost`.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - State<T> passed by pointer to a function that mutates it
//   - same src/dst layout (the function returns the same state type)
//     → the e-graph's `state_transform_elision` rule rewrites the
//     transform to its input (no-op)

layout Point = { x: u32, y: u32 }

fn set_x(p: State<Point>, v: u32) {
    p.x = v;
    return;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let p = state_new(Point);
    set_x(p, 7);
    return p.x;
}
